---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import profile from '../../data/agile-profile.json';
import t from '../../data/tariffs.json';

const OCTOPUS_REFERRAL = 'https://share.octopus.energy/lush-spark-49';
const AXLE_REFERRAL = 'https://vpp.axle.energy/landing?ref=R-QADMOHUO';

const { current: cur, comparison: cmp } = t;
const elec = cur.electricity;
const prev = cur.previous_electricity;
const gbp = (n: number) => `£${Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-GB')}`;
const ukDate = (iso: string) =>
  new Date(iso + 'T12:00:00Z').toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric' });
const chosen = cmp.rows.find((r: any) => r.chosen);
const left = cmp.rows.find((r: any) => r.previous);
const switchSaving = left.net - chosen.net;

const slots = profile.slots as number[];
const lo = Math.min(...slots);
const hi = Math.max(...slots);
const slotTime = (i: number) => `${String(Math.floor(i / 2)).padStart(2, '0')}:${i % 2 ? '30' : '00'}`;
// 16:00–19:00 is the evening peak the battery exists to dodge; ink it so it reads as the villain.
const isPeak = (i: number) => i >= 32 && i < 38;
const shade = (v: number) => {
  const k = (v - lo) / (hi - lo);
  if (k > 0.72) return '#5b83e6';
  if (k > 0.45) return '#9ab4f2';
  if (k > 0.22) return '#c9d8f8';
  return '#e8eefc';
};
const cheapest = slots.indexOf(lo);
const dearest = slots.indexOf(hi);

const barMax = Math.max(...cmp.rows.map((r: any) => r.net));
---

<Base
  title="The tariff"
  description="What this UK solar, battery and Predbat home actually pays for electricity, why a plain 12-month fix is the wrong tariff once a heat pump and an EV arrive, and the modelling behind the switch to Intelligent Octopus Go."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>The tariff</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The tariff decides what each stored kWh is worth, so it matters about as much as the
      hardware does. For the first six months we were on a
      {' '}<strong>plain 12-month fix at {prev.unit_p}p</strong>. It was already a poor fit for
      this house and would have got worse once the heat pump arrived. On 14 September we moved to
      {' '}<strong>{elec.product}</strong>. Below is what we're on now, what we left, and the
      modelling behind the decision.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>What we're on now</h2></div>
  <table class="spec">
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Import</td>
        <td>
          <strong>{elec.product}</strong> — <strong>{elec.night_p}p/kWh</strong> for
          {' '}{elec.night_hours} hours ({elec.night_window}), {elec.day_p}p the rest of the day,
          standing charge {elec.standing_p}p/day. Started {ukDate(elec.started)}, runs to
          {' '}{ukDate(elec.ends)}. Replaced a {prev.unit_p}p flat fix we left on
          {' '}{ukDate(prev.left)}, five months early.
          <span class="mono fine"> {elec.tariff_code}</span>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Gas</td>
        <td>
          <strong>{cur.gas.product}</strong> — {cur.gas.unit_p}p/kWh, standing charge
          {' '}{cur.gas.standing_p}p/day. {cur.gas.note}
          <span class="mono fine"> {cur.gas.tariff_code}</span>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Export</td>
        <td>
          <strong>{cur.export.product}</strong> — flat {cur.export.unit_p}p/kWh at any time of day.
          We chose it because the rate is guaranteed.
          <strong> Paying since 22 September 2026</strong>, after three and a half months of
          exporting for nothing. <span class="mono fine">{cur.export.tariff_code}</span>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>VPP</td>
        <td>
          <strong>{cur.vpp.product}</strong> — £{cur.vpp.rate_gbp_per_kwh}/kWh with a
          £{cur.vpp.monthly_minimum_gbp}/month minimum. It settles through the smart meter, so it was
          paying for months before the export certification came through. <a href="/stats/#axle">What it's paid us so far →</a>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p><strong>We gave our export away for 110 days.</strong> The panels exported
    {' '}<strong>{cur.export.kwh_given_away.toLocaleString('en-GB')} kWh</strong> between
    {' '}{ukDate(cur.export.since)} and {ukDate(cur.export.given_away_until)} and earned <strong>£0</strong> for it,
    because the export MPAN hadn't been registered. At {cur.export.unit_p}p that was
    {' '}<strong>{gbp(cur.export.value_forgone_gbp)}</strong>. The MPAN came through on 14 September
    and the tariff started paying on 22 September. The {' '}<a href="/stats/">stats</a> show £0 export
    revenue for every day before that, since we weren't paid anything. If you're having solar fitted,
    sort the export paperwork out first and worry about optimising later.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note quiet">
    <p><strong>VAT on electricity drops from 5% to 0% on 1 October 2026.</strong> Every rate on this
    page falls by about 4.8% without us doing anything. It applies to every tariff, so none of the
    comparisons change, but the figures above are the last ones that include VAT.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>Why the fix stopped working</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    A flat {prev.unit_p}p is fine for a house that just uses electricity. With a battery it makes
    less sense, and with {' '}<a href="/ev/">an EV</a> and <a href="/heat-pump/">a heat pump</a> on
    the way it makes very little. When every hour costs the same, the battery can't buy cheap and
    use later, and the heat pump can't be run in cheaper hours. All the new demand would have been
    paid for at the full rate, which is why we left early and paid the exit fee.
  </p>

  <div class="card" style="margin-top:1rem">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h2>Modelled annual cost, heat pump and car both running</h2>
      <span class="meta">export at 12p · electricity VAT at 0%</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tar-bars">
      {cmp.rows.map((r: any) => (
        <div class="trow">
          <span class="tlabel">
            {r.chosen || r.current ? <strong>{r.name}</strong> : r.name}
            <span class="tverdict">{r.verdict}</span>
          </span>
          <span class="ttrack">
            <span
              class={`tfill ${r.chosen ? 'accent' : r.current ? 'dark' : 'mid'}`}
              style={`width:${(r.net / barMax) * 100}%`}
            ></span>
          </span>
          <span class="tval">{gbp(r.net)}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      Staying on the fix would have cost <strong>{gbp(switchSaving)} a year</strong> more. The
      average import price drops from {left.blended_p}p to {chosen.blended_p}p, because the battery
      and the {elec.night_hours}-hour cheap window move most of the year's demand off the day rate.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>What we moved to: {elec.product}</h2></div>
  <table class="spec">
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Night</td><td><strong>{elec.night_p}p/kWh</strong>, {elec.night_window} — {elec.night_hours} hours, and it covers the whole house as well as the car. There are also occasional smart dispatch slots outside the window.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Day</td><td>{elec.day_p}p/kWh. That's expensive, so the aim is to buy as little as possible at this rate. The battery and the heat pump's thermal store are what let us do that.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Standing</td><td>{elec.standing_p}p/day.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Timing</td><td>Live from <strong>{ukDate(elec.started)}</strong>, a few months before the heat pump goes in in December. If we'd waited for the fix to end in March 2027, we'd have spent a whole heating season on the wrong tariff.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>A correction on Agile.</strong> An earlier version of this page said Agile came out
    about £100/yr cheaper than Intelligent Octopus Go. That was a bug in our model. The battery
    charging logic stopped looking ahead when it reached another half hour at the same price, so in
    a flat six-hour window the battery never filled up. Once that was fixed and the battery charged
    fully overnight, a guaranteed {elec.night_hours} hours at {elec.night_p}p beat Agile by
    {' '}{gbp(cmp.rows[2].net - chosen.net)} a year, and the high day rate mattered much less.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note quiet">
    <p><strong>Cosy Octopus is Octopus's heat pump tariff, and it came fourth.</strong> Its
    off-peak rate is around 14.5p against IOG's 8p, and its 16:00–19:00 peak is over 50p, which is
    when a heat pump works hardest on a cold evening. Much of what Cosy offers is flexibility that a
    15 kWh battery already gives you. If you have a battery, do the sums before assuming the heat
    pump tariff is the right one.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="cols-2" style="margin-top:1rem">
    <div class="card referral">
      <p class="kicker">Thinking of switching?</p>
      <h2 style="margin:0.2rem 0 0"><img src="/octopus-mark.png" alt="" class="octopus-logo-inline" /> Octopus · we both get £50</h2>
      <p>The site has no sponsors. The referral links are the only money it makes, and you get the
      same amount we do. You don't need solar; it works for any tariff.</p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn octopus" href={OCTOPUS_REFERRAL} rel="noopener"><img src="/octopus-mark.png" alt="Octopus Energy" class="octopus-mark" /> Claim £50</a>
        <p class="fine mono">share.octopus.energy/lush-spark-49</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card referral">
      <p class="kicker">Got a battery already?</p>
      <h2 style="margin:0.2rem 0 0"><img src="/axle-logo-black.png" alt="" class="axle-logo-inline" /> Axle · we both get £25</h2>
      <p>Paid at £1/kWh for each grid-balancing event, with a £10 monthly minimum. In events-only
      mode Predbat runs the battery the rest of the time. <a href="/stats/#axle">What it's paid us so far →</a></p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn axle" href={AXLE_REFERRAL} rel="noopener"><img src="/axle-logo-black.png" alt="Axle" class="axle-mark" /> Claim £25</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>The half-hourly price we modelled against</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    We're not on Agile, but a year of its published half-hourly rates is a good free record of when
    electricity is expensive. Every comparison above was modelled against it.
  </p>

  <section class="card">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h2>A typical day's Agile import price</h2>
      <span class="meta">mean of a full year of published rates · Jun 2025 → Jun 2026</span>
    </div>
    <div class="price-curve">
      {slots.map((v, i) => (
        <span
          class="bar"
          style={`height:${((v - lo * 0.72) / (hi - lo * 0.72)) * 100}%; background:${isPeak(i) ? 'var(--dark)' : shade(v)}`}
          title={`${slotTime(i)} — ${v.toFixed(2)}p`}
        ></span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="price-axis"><span>00:00</span><span>06:00</span><span>12:00</span><span>18:00</span><span>23:30</span></div>
    <p class="caption">
      A battery charges in the pale hours and runs the house through the dark 16:00–19:00 peak.
      Averaged over the year, the cheapest half hour is
      <strong>{lo.toFixed(2)}p at {slotTime(cheapest)}</strong> and the dearest
      <strong>{hi.toFixed(2)}p at {slotTime(dearest)}</strong>, with a mean of
      {' '}<strong>{profile.mean_p_per_kwh}p</strong>. Intelligent Octopus Go gives us the same cheap
      overnight hours at a fixed price, so we don't depend on the forecast.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>What happened to Flux</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    The original business case assumed <strong>Octopus Flux</strong>, the usual battery tariff,
    with a cheap overnight window and a 30p evening export peak. The plan was to charge cheaply
    overnight and export from the battery in the 4pm to 7pm peak. In <strong>March 2026</strong>,
    months before the panels went up, Flux and Intelligent Flux closed to new customers. Standard
    Flux has since reopened. Intelligent Flux was still closed as of August 2026.
  </p>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p><strong>Lesson one:</strong> don't base a payback calculation that runs for years on one
    tariff staying available. Octopus can withdraw a tariff whenever it likes, so model your
    second choice as well. We hadn't, and re-planning in a hurry wasn't much fun.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="lede">
    Losing Flux's 30p export peak cost us less than we expected. Instead of exporting at the peak
    rate, the battery avoids importing at the peak rate, and that's worth about the same. With a
    heat pump and a car on the way, avoiding import is also the more useful thing for the battery
    to do, so we're staying off Flux even though it has reopened. Its cheap import window is only
    about three hours, which isn't enough for a 45 kWh winter day plus a car.
  </p>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>Why we're on flat 12p export</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    Agile Outgoing pays a half-hourly export price, uncapped, with no exit fees. We ran a year of
    real rates against our own generation. Flat 12p comes to {gbp(t.export_comparison.outgoing_fixed_12p)} and Agile Outgoing to {gbp(t.export_comparison.agile_outgoing)}, an average of only {t.export_comparison.agile_achieved_p}p. That's {gbp(t.export_comparison.outgoing_fixed_12p - t.export_comparison.agile_outgoing)} a year in favour of flat 12p. Agile
    only pays more than 12p between 16:00 and 19:00, and this house generates a lot and uses
    little, so most of our export happens at midday when Agile pays 5–7p. The heat pump will widen
    the gap. It will use up the spring and autumn surplus, which is when Agile Outgoing pays best,
    and leave the summer midday surplus, when it pays worst.
  </p>

  <div class="note quiet">
    <p><strong>What that means for the battery:</strong> an Axle VPP event at £1/kWh comes first,
    then using the energy in the house instead of importing, then exporting at 12p. Predbat is set
    up with those priorities and plans the day around them. One result surprised us. On
    {' '}{elec.product} at {elec.night_p}p, imported power costs less than the 12p we'd get for
    exporting our own solar, so it makes sense to <em>sell</em> the solar and charge the car
    overnight. See <a href="/ev/">the car</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <style>
    .price-curve {
      display: flex;
      align-items: flex-end;
      gap: 2px;
      height: 190px;
      margin-top: 0.5rem;
    }
    .price-curve .bar { flex: 1; border-radius: 2px 2px 0 0; min-height: 3px; }
    .price-axis {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-between;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      color: var(--ink-400);
      margin-top: 0.4rem;
    }
    .tar-bars { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.6rem; margin-top: 0.4rem; }
    .trow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 15rem 1fr 4rem; align-items: center; gap: 0.8rem; }
    .tlabel { font-size: 0.86rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
    .tverdict { font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--ink-400); }
    .ttrack { background: var(--track); border-radius: 999px; height: 12px; overflow: hidden; }
    .tfill { display: block; height: 100%; border-radius: 999px; min-width: 6px; background: #9ab4f2; }
    .tfill.accent { background: var(--accent); }
    .tfill.dark { background: var(--dark); }
    .tfill.mid { background: #9ab4f2; }
    .tval { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.78rem; text-align: right; color: var(--ink-400); }
    @media (max-width: 620px) {
      .trow { grid-template-columns: 1fr 4rem; }
      .tlabel { grid-column: 1 / -1; margin-bottom: -0.3rem; }
    }
  </style>
</Base>
